# AGENT.md — src/features/

One directory per feature (use-case). Each is a self-contained vertical slice that
cuts through all layers.

## Call direction

```
controller  →  handler  →  repository
```

A layer only ever calls the one directly below it. Never let a controller
reach past the handler into the repository, and never let a repository call
back up into a handler.

## Responsibilities

- `<feature>.module.ts` — NestJS wiring for this slice. Exports the handler
  so other slices can consume it.
- `<feature>.controller.ts` — HTTP only: routing, params, status codes.
  Delegates all logic to the handler. No business logic here.
- `<feature>.handler.ts` — all business logic. This is where rules,
  validation of invariants, and orchestration live. One class per feature,
  one method per use-case. No HTTP concepts (no Request/Response types).
- `<feature>.repository.ts` — data access only. No business decisions.
- `<feature>.entity.ts` — the domain model{{#if hasOrmClassEntity}} (also the persistence
  model, decorated for {{orm}}){{/if}}.
- `create-<feature>.dto.ts` — request shapes{{#if hasValidation}} with {{validation}} rules{{/if}}.
- `<feature>.response.ts` — output shapes returned by the handler.

## Rules

- A feature may import from `src/shared/`. A feature must **not** import from
  another feature's internals — go through the other feature's exported
  handler, and register the dependency in `<feature>.module.ts`.
- New feature means: create the directory with all eight files, then register
  it in `src/app.module.ts`. A feature that isn't registered silently does nothing.
- Keep the controller thin — one line per endpoint that delegates to the handler.
  If you find yourself writing business logic in the controller, move it to the handler.
- Cross-cutting concerns (config, database, auth, cache, messaging) live in
  `src/shared/` — never duplicate infrastructure in feature folders.
